<h3>Warranty terms that actually matter</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Carcass:</strong> 5–10 years standard on BWP-ply construction; premium builders offer up to lifetime against manufacturing defects. This is the number that tells you what the maker thinks of their own plywood.</li>
  <li><strong>Hardware mechanisms:</strong> the hidden gem — Blum, Hettich, and Häfele publish cycle-tested guarantees on runners, hinges, and lift systems, in many lines effectively lifetime. Ensure your invoice names the brand and series so brand warranty is claimable.</li>
  <li><strong>Finishes:</strong> 1–5 years against peeling/delamination depending on finish type; acrylic and PU usually carry longer cover than budget laminates.</li>
  <li><strong>Installation workmanship:</strong> 1 year standard — covers alignment drift, soft-close failures, and fitting issues.</li>
  <li><strong>The standard exclusions:</strong> water damage from unreported leaks, overloading beyond rated capacity, and DIY modifications. This is exactly why the under-sink cabinet's moisture detailing (section 9) protects your warranty as much as your plywood.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Get it in writing, itemised</span>A single line saying "10-year warranty" is marketing. A real warranty document lists <strong>carcass, hardware (by brand and series), finish, and workmanship separately</strong>, each with its period, claim process, and exclusions. Ask for exactly that — reputable firms hand it over without hesitation.</div>
<h2>15.Common Mistakes &amp; the Maintenance That Prevents Regret</h2>
<h3>The 8 mistakes we see most</h3>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Ignoring corners</strong> — a plain shelf in a corner cabinet forfeits its volume forever.</li>
  <li><strong>Too few drawers</strong> — shelving the base cabinets to save money is the falsest economy in kitchens.</li>
  <li><strong>Unplanned pantry</strong> — dry goods scattered across five cabinets instead of one zone.</li>
  <li><strong>Cheap unbranded channels</strong> — the first thing to sag, stick, and fail.</li>
  <li><strong>Style over function</strong> — handle-less gloss everywhere, but nowhere sensible for the pressure cooker.</li>
  <li><strong>No ventilation</strong> — sealed cabinets for onions/potatoes, or appliance towers without airflow gaps.</li>
  <li><strong>Wrong heights</strong> — wall units hung too high for the primary cook; measure for the person, not the catalogue.</li>
  <li><strong>Underestimating capacity</strong> — design for your groceries after a monthly shop, not the empty-kitchen photo.</li>
</ol>
<h3>The 10-minute monthly maintenance routine</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Wipe channels dry and clear crumbs from runners (never oil ball-bearing channels — it attracts grit).</li>
  <li>Check and hand-tighten hinge screws on the most-used doors.</li>
  <li>Empty and dry the under-sink cabinet; look for the first signs of dampness.</li>
  <li>Rotate pantry stock front-to-back; wipe container bases.</li>
  <li>Annually: get the installer to realign shutters and tune soft-close — usually covered in year one, cheap thereafter, and it doubles the "new kitchen feel" lifespan.</li>
</ul>
<h2>16.What's New in 2026 (and What to Skip)</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Worth adopting:</strong> handle-less push-to-open systems (now reliable), in-drawer lighting with motion sensors, plinth/skirting drawers reclaiming the dead 100mm under base units, and hidden "pocket door" storage walls that conceal the working kitchen in open layouts.</li>
  <li><strong>Genuinely sustainable:</strong> FSC-certified plywood, low-emission (E0/E1) boards, and modularity itself — replacing one worn module instead of one whole kitchen.</li>
  <li><strong>Wait and watch:</strong> motorized servo-drive drawers and sensor-opening cabinets — delightful in demos, but in Indian conditions they add failure points and cost far ahead of the convenience gained. Buy the mechanical version of everything first.</li>
</ul>
<h2>17.Expert Recommendations: The Shortlists</h2>
<h3>Every kitchen, regardless of size or budget</h3>
<p>Tandem drawers in the prep zone, one deep pot drawer, a cutlery organizer, a waste-bin pull-out, and one solved corner (if the layout has one). This five-item core is where function per rupee peaks.</p>
<h3>Best for small kitchens</h3>
<p>Tall units to the ceiling, a 270° carousel or magic corner, plinth drawers, door-mounted racks, and a rolling-shutter appliance garage — small kitchens win by using space nobody counts.</p>
<h3>Best for large kitchens</h3>
<p>A full pull-out pantry, LeMans corners, dedicated appliance towers, an island drawer bank, and a separate serving-zone cabinet with plate organizers near the dining side.</p>
<h3>Best premium picks</h3>
<p>Blum Aventos lift-ups, Hettich/Blum tandem boxes at 50–70kg rating, a LeMans unit, and in-drawer sensor lighting — the four upgrades whose quality you feel every single day.</p>
<h3>Best budget picks</h3>
<p>Ebco tandem drawers, a carousel corner, wire-basket grocery tall unit, and modular drawer inserts added over time — 80% of the function at 40% of the price.</p>
